ligned}c & =F(4) \\\\  & =P(Y\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2+0.4+0.1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.3+0.9+1=2.2.\n$$" 13194139535677 true] [17592186129820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535677 true] [17592186129820 1100 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(4 < X \\le 6)$.\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139535677 false] [17592186129820 1100 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(4<X\\le 6).$\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139535677 true] [17592186129820 1145 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $X$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 6) & =F(6)-F(4) \\\\  & =0.9-0.4 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535677 true] [17592186129821 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535677 true] [17592186129821 1100 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2} \\le Y \\le \\frac{3}{2})$.\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139535677 false] [17592186129821 1100 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139535677 true] [17592186129821 1145 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<Y\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a\\le Y\\le b,$ not $a<Y\\le b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ to the form $a<Y\\le b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y$ is equivalent to $0<Y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) & =P(0<Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{3}{2})-F(0) \\\\  & =0.8-0.1 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535677 true] [17592186129822 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535677 true] [17592186129822 1100 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X > 4)$.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139535677 false] [17592186129822 1100 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(X>4).$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139535677 true] [17592186129822 1145 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-